Guided Exercise: Externalize Configuration of Applications
Goal of the Exercise
Deploy a web server and configure it using files stored in a ConfigMap instead of storing them inside the container image.
Outcomes
You will learn how to:
1.	Create a deployment
2.	Expose the deployment using service and route
3.	Create a ConfigMap from files
4.	Mount the ConfigMap as a volume
________________________________________
Step 1: Prepare the Lab Environment
Command:
lab start storage-configs
What this does
This command:
•	Starts the OpenShift cluster lab
•	Creates required project (namespace)
•	Prepares files needed for the exercise
Example files created:
index.html
redhatlogo.png
These will later be stored in the ConfigMap.
________________________________________
Step 2: Login to OpenShift Web Console
Open browser:
https://console-openshift-console.apps.ocp4.example.com
Login:
Username: developer
Password: developer
Then switch perspective:
Administrator
Why Administrator view?
Because it allows you to manage:
•	Deployments
•	Services
•	Routes
•	ConfigMaps
________________________________________
Step 3: Create Web Application Deployment
Navigate:
Workloads → Deployments → Create Deployment
Fill values:
Field	Value
Deployment Name	webconfig
Image	registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/ubi9/httpd-24:latest
Project	storage-configs
Container Image Used
ubi9/httpd-24
This is:
Red Hat Universal Base Image with Apache HTTP server
It runs a web server on port 8080.
________________________________________
What happens after deployment?
OpenShift creates:
Deployment
ReplicaSet
Pods
Pods run Apache HTTPD web server.
Wait until:
3 pods running
Example:
webconfig-xxxxx
webconfig-yyyyy
webconfig-zzzzz
________________________________________
Step 4: Expose the Application (Service + Route)
Pods cannot be accessed directly.
So we create:
1️⃣ Service → internal access
2️⃣ Route → external access
________________________________________
Step 5: Create Service
Navigate:
Networking → Services → Create Service
Fill:
Field	Value
Service Name	webconfig-svc
App Selector	webconfig
Port	8080
Target Port	8080
________________________________________
What Service Does
Service acts as a load balancer for pods.
User → Service → Pod
Even if pods change, the service IP remains constant.
________________________________________
CLI Alternative
oc expose deploy/webconfig \
--namespace storage-configs \
--name webconfig-svc \
--selector app=webconfig \
--target-port 8080 \
--port 8080
Command Explanation
Part	Meaning
oc expose	create service
deploy/webconfig	deployment to expose
--namespace	project
--name	service name
--selector	selects pods
--target-port	container port
--port	service port
________________________________________
Step 6: Create Route (External Access)
Navigate:
Networking → Routes → Create Route
Fill values:
Field	Value
Route Name	webconfig-rt
Service	webconfig-svc
Target Port	8080
________________________________________
What Route Does
Route exposes application outside the cluster.
Flow:
User Browser
     ↓
Route
     ↓
Service
     ↓
Pods
________________________________________
CLI Alternative
oc expose svc/webconfig-svc \
--namespace storage-configs \
--name webconfig-rt
Explanation:
Part	Meaning
svc	service
oc expose	create route
--name	route name
________________________________________
Step 7: Access Web Application
Open route link.
You will see:
Apache HTTP Test Page
This is the default page inside container image.
