Step 8: Create ConfigMap from Files
Navigate:
Workloads → ConfigMaps → Create ConfigMap
Set name:
webfiles
________________________________________
Add Data File
Key:
index.html
Value file:
/home/student/DO180/labs/storage-configs/index.html
________________________________________
Add Binary Data File
Key:
redhatlogo.png
Value file:
/home/student/DO180/labs/storage-configs/redhatlogo.png
________________________________________
Why Binary Data?
Images like:
.png
.jpg
are binary files, so they go under:
binaryData
________________________________________
ConfigMap Structure
It will look like:
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: webfiles
data:
  index.html: <html content>

binaryData:
  redhatlogo.png: <base64 encoded image>
________________________________________
Step 9: Mount ConfigMap as Volume
Login to cluster:
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
________________________________________
Select project:
oc project storage-configs
________________________________________
Mount ConfigMap:
oc set volume deployment/webconfig \
--add \
--type configmap \
--configmap-name webfiles \
--name webfiles-vol \
--mount-path /var/www/html/
________________________________________
Command Explanation
Option	Meaning
oc set volume	modify deployment volume
--add	add new volume
--type configmap	source is ConfigMap
--configmap-name webfiles	ConfigMap name
--name webfiles-vol	volume name
--mount-path	container directory
________________________________________
What Happens Now
Files from ConfigMap are mounted into container:
/var/www/html/
Example inside container:
/var/www/html/index.html
/var/www/html/redhatlogo.png
This directory is Apache web server root directory.
So Apache serves these files.
________________________________________
